 "notes": "Him walking RIGHT in profile, both hands on the bouquet. We see his RIGHT side: near leg (full colour) = RIGHT, far leg (darker, behind) = LEFT. 12-frame cycle: frame 0 = right heel strike (right foot forward), 6 = left heel strike; 0/6 contact, 1/7 down (lowest), 3/9 passing, 4/10 up (highest). The 24-frame strip has the same phases at double the index. RECOMMENDED: walk-strip-24 at 20 fps with the position snapped to the frame grid (see 'recommended'). Registration: every cell has the hip pivot at (hipX, hipY[frame]) and the ground (bottom of the planted near sole) at groundY; the far foot plants on farGroundY (10 px higher = depth, as in him-side.png). Draw a cell so its (hipX, groundY) lands on his ground point; scale = desiredHeight/604 (cells are at him-side.png pixel scale). Hip x is constant in the cell and a planted foot moves back exactly strideCycle/frames px per frame, so frame = floor(d/strideCycle*frames) % frames keeps planted feet still when the sprite is drawn at x0 + floor(d/(strideCycle/frames))*(strideCycle/frames) (snapped). Moving the sprite continuously instead makes a planted foot creep up to strideCycle/frames px per held frame. Pace: speed = 240 sprite px/s (strideCycle 288 per 1.2 s, two steps). START: play walk-start-strip at its fps from the standing pose (its frame 0 IS walk-stand), drawing each frame with the hip at standX + start.dx[i]; then continue with cycle frame 0 at standX + start.joinDx (73) and keep walking (x0 = standX + joinDx, d = 0). STOP: end the walk on a heel strike - cycle frame 0 (right foot forward; 24-strip frame 0) -> play walk-stop-r-strip, or cycle frame 6 (left foot forward; 24-strip frame 12) -> play walk-stop-l-strip - drawing frame i with the hip at (where that cycle frame was drawn) + dx[i]. Feet stay planted throughout. The last stop frame IS walk-stand (pixel-identical to him-side.png, the far leg dissolves into the source's own far leg over the last frames), so no cross-fade is needed. Sequences are 20 fps; for 10 fps play only the indices in frames10fps. feet[] gives per-frame sole centre x, heel/toe x, lift above its ground and a planted flag (for contact shadows). walk-strip (12) at 10 fps is the light-weight fallback."
}